---
/**
 * The row of product-type tiles that leads the catalogue: "Tutti", then one
 * tile per type the shop can answer.
 *
 * Matched to the reference site's own strip (miamedicalitalia.it, measured
 * 2026-09-05, desktop and 390px): a picture over a bold name, tiles of equal
 * width sharing the row, and the selected one filled rather than ticked.
 *
 * THE NAMES ARE INK AT `font-semibold`, going accent only on hover — one step
 * under the category tiles below, which are content and stay bold. This strip
 * is chrome that filters them, and chrome shouting as loudly as the thing it
 * filters is what made the whole page read as bold (owner, 2026-09-05). The reference paints all
 * nine accent-blue, and that is what made the strip shout over the directory
 * three rows below it (owner, 2026-09-05) — nine blue headings above eight
 * black ones reads as two competing shops. One tile carries accent ink here:
 * the selected one, where the colour IS the marker.
 *
 * SELECTED IS THE `accent-tint` FILL, ACCENT INK, AND A SOFT ACCENT HAIRLINE —
 * `border-accent/30`, not the solid accent the reference draws at 2px. The
 * fill and the ink do the marking; the border only stops the tint from floating
 * edgelessly among eight bordered cards. At full strength it read as a hard
 * frame, which is not how anything else on this site marks a choice (owner,
 * 2026-09-05).
 *
 * Unselected is a white fill on one hairline, the same card the category
 * directory uses, so the two grids read as one family.
 *
 * NO TINT WELL. The art sits straight on the card, which is what the category
 * tiles below already do and for the same reason: these icons carry their own
 * white ground, and `mix-blend-multiply` drops it into the card. The reference
 * frames each one in a bordered 64px well, and copying that put THREE nested
 * rectangles in every tile — the card, the well, and the blue frame baked into
 * the icon itself — nine times across one row. That is what made the strip look
 * "messy, cards gathered" (owner, 2026-09-05). One border per tile.
 *
 * IT SCROLLS SIDEWAYS ON A PHONE AND SHARES THE ROW ON DESKTOP. Nine tiles wrap
 * to three rows at 390px and stand 300px tall before a single product, so below
 * `mid` this is one scrolling strip that bleeds to both screen edges — the clip
 * at the viewport IS the affordance, exactly as the filter row's pills do it.
 * The explicit width on the scroll port is load-bearing for the same reason it
 * is there: this row's parent is a wrapping flex column, which would otherwise
 * size the port to all nine tiles laid out flat and scroll the document
 * sideways instead of the strip.
 *
 * Above `mid` the tiles take `flex-1` between a 112px floor and a 144px ceiling
 * and the row WRAPS, so a shop that grows a tenth type gets a second row rather
 * than nine tiles squeezed under their own labels.
 *
 * THE NAMES ARE 14px AT `font-medium` (owner, 2026-09-05, set in the browser
 * and measured there). This strip is chrome, and it takes the header nav's
 * scale rather than the body's — nine names in a row read as a legend under
 * nine pictures, not as nine headings, and at 16px semibold they were louder
 * than the category tiles they filter. Two lines are reserved either way, so a
 * one-word type and a three-word one hang from the same baseline.
 */
import { mediaUrl } from '~/lib/api';
import { localeForRequest, translate } from '~/lib/i18n';
import type { ProductTypeTile } from '~/lib/product-type-nav';

interface Props {
  tiles: ProductTypeTile[];
}

const { tiles } = Astro.props;
const locale = localeForRequest();
---

<nav
  class="max-mid:-mx-gutter max-mid:w-[calc(100%+2*var(--spacing-gutter))] max-mid:px-gutter max-mid:overflow-x-auto mid:flex-wrap mid:pb-6.5 max-mid:pb-3 flex [scrollbar-width:none] gap-3.5 [&::-webkit-scrollbar]:hidden"
  aria-label={translate(locale, 'catalog.type.filter')}
>
  {
    tiles.map((tile) => (
      <a
        class:list={[
          'group/type font-ui max-mid:w-31 mid:min-w-28 mid:max-w-36 mid:flex-1 mid:basis-0 rounded-field flex flex-none flex-col items-center gap-2.5 border px-3 pt-3.5 pb-3.5 text-center no-underline',
          tile.isActive
            ? 'bg-accent-tint border-accent/30 text-accent'
            : 'border-hair hover:border-hair-strong text-ink bg-white',
        ]}
        href={tile.href}
        aria-current={tile.isActive ? 'true' : undefined}
      >
        {/* A fixed height at every width, so the names below start on one line
            across the row whatever shape the art inside turns out to be. */}
        <span class="grid h-14 w-full flex-none place-items-center" aria-hidden="true">
          {tile.imagePath ? (
            /* CAPPED IN PIXELS, and `max-h-14` has to keep step with the `h-14`
               above. Neither `size-full` nor `max-h-full` works here: a centred
               grid item is sized by its CONTENT, so the row it sits in is
               auto-sized, so a percentage against it is circular and resolves to
               `auto` — which let a 500px icon draw at full size straight through
               the label below. */
            <img
              class="block max-h-14 max-w-full object-contain mix-blend-multiply"
              src={mediaUrl(tile.imagePath)}
              alt=""
              loading="lazy"
            />
          ) : (
            /* "Tutti" has no category to borrow a picture from, and the grid
               glyph is what the catalogue is marked with everywhere else. */
            <svg
              class="text-ink-2 size-7"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              fill="none"
              stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="2"
              stroke-linecap="round"
            >
              <rect x="3" y="3" width="7" height="7" rx="1" />
              <rect x="14" y="3" width="7" height="7" rx="1" />
              <rect x="3" y="14" width="7" height="7" rx="1" />
              <rect x="14" y="14" width="7" height="7" rx="1" />
            </svg>
          )}
        </span>
        {/* Two lines reserved, as the product card reserves two for its name:
            a one-word type and a three-word one then hang from the same
            baseline instead of floating at different heights down the row. */}
        <span class="min-h-[2.6em] text-sm/[1.3] font-medium group-hover/type:text-accent">
          {tile.label}
        </span>
      </a>
    ))
  }
</nav>
